Nvidia earns a big profit whenever it sells a GPU. Whoever buys that chip, however, does not incur an immediate cost because the purchase is treated as capital spending. Thus, while aggregate cash flows do not change, reported earnings still rise. This has pushed up profits margins in the tech sector.

@azeem Yes, which is why capex/GDP and corporate profits tend to move together.

Data centers are somewhat different in that they employ so few people. An $8 billion datacenter could employ only 100 people once it is up and running. That's $80 million per worker. The capital-labor ratio in the economy is $700,000. So, the capital-labor ratio for data centers is more than 100x that of the broader economy. This has the effect of pulling down the labor share, leading to higher aggregate profits at the economy-wide level.

